this started out as a short that Gillian  made with Jenny right in what year was  that when we shot the short in 2009 four  in four days with no money I was sitting  in the editing room with my editor and  we had a rough cut of 30 minutes and it  was amazing  Jenny's performance just blew me away  you know we didn't watch dailies because  it was a short and I you know wasn't  fine-tuned director yet and I was  watching all the footage putting it  together  and the performance just was so stunning  and the way Jenny as Donna navigated  between a range of like guttural laughs  and heartbreaking moments I just knew  this needed to be told in a bigger way  because of the performance and also I  wanted the story to be told in a bigger  way but we didn't have the money and we  didn't have the story yet and we all had  to go off and be grownups and and our  careers had to you know mature and Jenny  moved to LA and I stayed in New York and  I met Elizabeth at a mixer I had a IFP  which is like a great program because it  brought Elizabeth and I together and  that's sort of where the the feature was  was born I had an early draft of the  script and I was looking to find a  partner to help me get this made and Liz  was that partner the development process  was really just working on the script  for a long time figuring out Donna's  character turning her into a stand-up  comedian getting to know her family her  friends her world trying to find  conflict when we never wanted the  conflict to be will she or won't she go  through with the abortion you know it's  a complex emotional experience but it's  we don't want that to be the question we  always knew it would be you know a  decisive moment for her and so it was  really about will she or won't she have  the courage to let someone in to connect  to someone to be honest with herself and  with him it's really developing that the  relationship with Max and looking to  some of our favorite romantic comedies  of all time and and really using those  tropes but kind of infusing it with this  modern twist  and so yeah we financing was sort of the  most boring part of what I do but like a  mix of equity and grants and Kickstarter  and the tax credit and you name it we  did it and it actually didn't take very  long at all I think we found the right  partners who were really eager to make  this happen and to be part of this crazy  family and and then we shot for 18 days  in Brooklyn and Manhattan didn't like my  boyfriend's apartment our friend's  apartments with the support of Planned  Parenthood we shot in a Planned  Parenthood didn't in Westchester and  yeah we were in post sort of all summer  and fall Gillian and I both were working  day jobs I used to run the phone program  for Kickstarter for three years and  Gillian was at the Directors Guild of  America and we took some time off for  prep and production but other than that  we were just totally ruining our  boyfriend's lives and being workaholics  do you anticipate the conversation about  it changing in any way when it starts to  open in redder States and it's opened in  so far probably that makes sense to me I  think what we can do is to keep our  voice steady and keep moving in one  direction which is of course we are  pro-choice and want to be part of a  movement that you know helps everybody  make choice that's right for them and  secures those rights but I I've been  thinking about this a lot because in  general I'm very uncomfortable with  confrontation  I don't get in a lot of arguments with  my friends my parents when I have fights  with my husband  they really bum me out for like a long  long time I recently heard that if you  heard a pig's feelings that it will have  hurt feelings for like two weeks and but  I can't avoid it you know we made this  movie and I stand by it and as we as the  script was being developed and I started  to think more and more about it and  watch what was going on in our country  found myself really wanting to have a  voice in in the I hate  call it a fight but it is it it's a  struggle but it seems like there's one  side that it seems like there are like  some rules laid down about how we should  fight this fight and I just reject that  because I I think you can say things and  do things in a way that is earnest but  also strong and doesn't have to be  bitter and you know like the second you  see someone's fiscal up you punch them  in the face and when when we were in  Dallas there was a woman that stood up  at the end of the very very positive and  you know really fervent Q&amp;A that was you  know really excited about the film and  she was upset about the way that one of  the jokes went down in the movie and she  asked a question about it you know I  don't agree with her opinion at all but  the crowd was sort of really against her  and that honestly made me more  uncomfortable than her disagreeing with  us was just the inability to have a like  a conversation where people just have  good behavior and so I think for us we  were really thoughtful about this film I  know sometimes we step over the line but  I think that what makes something modern  is when it it's flexible with boundaries  that you know I think like you see that  in general in like literature you know  like it and I think it's important for  us to say we are a thoughtful playful  strong voice and you shouldn't  underestimate us but you should also  engage and we're here for a proper  useful conversation
 

